8cca8d034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124374 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124374 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the differential for $f(x,y) = x^2 \\sin y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ebce398-b228-51b0-8506-e0c6124478a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124374 #edb/ref 1074 "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124375 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6854" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124375 #edb/ref 1071 "Given a cone, find the differential $\\textrm d V$ for the volume $V$ of the cone if the radius is $r=6$ and the height is $h = 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124375 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb3ab0dc-7e26-58f5-a0c2-564c01c77f2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124375 #edb/ref 1074 "The volume of the cone is $V = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi r^2h.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(r,h) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂V}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi rh\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=6$ and $h=3,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(6,3) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot 6\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{1}{3}\\pi \\cdot {6}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =12\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+12\\pi \\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124376 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6875" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124376 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f (x, y) = xe^y ,$ use the differential $\\textrm d f$ to estimate the change $\\Delta f$ in the value of $f(x, y)$ from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c4816ed-fba0-5dc7-94ba-646b875d653d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124376 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(5,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(5,0) & ={e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x+5{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+5\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(5 + \\Delta x, 0 + \\Delta y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta f \\approx 1 \\, \\Delta x + 5 \\, \\Delta y.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-5=-3, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+5\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124377 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124377 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate $\\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)$ using a differential of the function $f(x,y) = \\sin x + \\cos y$ at the point $\\left( \\pi, 0 \\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "febc72d4-b1b8-52c9-a805-013b30695077" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124377 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $\\left( \\pi, 0 \\right),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\,\\text{d}x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(\\pi + \\Delta x, 0 + \\Delta y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta f \\approx  -1  \\,  \\Delta x + 0  \\,  \\Delta y\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)= f(3,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(3,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $(\\pi, 0)$ to $(3,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =3-\\pi ≈-0.141, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈-1(-0.141)+0(0.2) \\\\  & ≈0.141.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.141.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,0.2) & ≈f(\\pi ,0)+0.141 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.2) & ≈\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+0.141 \\\\  & ≈0+1+0.141 \\\\  & ≈1.141.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Differential of a Multivariable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124565 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f23c5c7-d281-54da-b625-412925c80667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592