/ref 17592186125481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124739 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124739 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124739 #edb/ref 1045 "Differentials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124739 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124739 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124739 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124739 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9e51b578-3c1e-53f2-bd6c-584e173a601f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125055 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125055 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125055 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125055 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(4\\pi ,8)$ to $(13,8.5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5a0b5eb-1d35-5e16-8d84-c218ac09143a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125055 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(4\\pi ,8),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(4\\pi ,8) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi \\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(4\\pi +Δx,8+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(4\\pi ,8)$ to $(13,8.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =13-4\\pi , \\\\ Δy & =8.5-8=0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (13-4\\pi )+1\\cdot (0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125057 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125057 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125057 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125057 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125057 #edb/ref 1071 "Given a cylinder, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the cylinder if the radius is $r=2$ and the height is $h=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125057 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35be243b-50e1-5aab-aebe-0bcb17e84ebe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125057 #edb/ref 1074 "The volume of the cylinder is $V=\\pi {r}^{2}h.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(r,h) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂V}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =2\\pi rh\\,\\text{d}r+\\pi {r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=2$ and $h=4,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(2,4) & =2\\pi \\cdot 2\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}r+\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =16\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+4\\pi \\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125226 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125226 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125226 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125226 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{x}+xz,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(0,1,2)$ to $(-1,0,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125226 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3d2ae44-10d9-5db3-858c-8da3c9096267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125226 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(y{e}^{x}+z)\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y+x\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,1,2),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,1,2) & =(1\\cdot {e}^{0}+2)\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,1+Δy,2+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈3\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,1,2)$ to $(-1,0,3),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =-1-0=-1, \\\\ Δy & =0-1=-1 \\\\ Δz & =3-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈3\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ decreases by approximately $-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125227 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125227 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125227 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125227 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate $(1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125227 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d809b121-2117-5794-bb89-efcea5057754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125227 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+2\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91)=f(1.81,0.91),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.81,0.91).$\n\nMoving from $(2,1)$ to $(1.81,0.91),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.81-2=-0.19, \\\\ Δy & =0.91-1=-0.09.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (-0.19)+2\\cdot (-0.09) \\\\  & ≈0-0.18 \\\\  & ≈-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.18.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.81,0.91) & ≈f(2,1)-0.18 \\\\ (1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91) & ≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-0.18 \\\\  & ≈0-0.18 \\\\  & ≈-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125477 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125477 #edb/ref 1061 25383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125477 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf1f5b6c-b40c-5ff9-9ed6-50ee5d23ff70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125477 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125478 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125478 #edb/ref 1061 25385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125478 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34d39ab5-5a0c-5548-9b37-4b9ff25664cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125478 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125479 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125479 #edb/ref 1061 25416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125479 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca5fbd6f-0bec-58b1-a606-32a0d7df8952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125479 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125480 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125480 #edb/ref 1061 25515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125480 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98241263-1948-5c19-9eb8-bdad86fba771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125480 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125481 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125481 #edb/ref 1061 25516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125481 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "382f7b0c-adae-5236-b08b-ef5b7008f906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125481 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125482 #edb/ref 1061 25517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125482 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "896f3d03-adff-53cf-be59-7b4310e8b280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125482 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125813 #edb/ref 1064 "The **differential** of a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ is denoted $\\textrm df(x,y)$ and represents the infinitesimal change in the function value that occurs when the inputs $x$ and $y$ are changed by infinitesimal amounts $\\textrm dx$ and $\\textrm dy.$\n\nThe formula for the differential comes from the multivariable chain rule, which can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the differential can be extended to any number of variables. For example, for a three-variable function $f(x,y,z),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125813 #edb/ref 1065 4191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125813 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "549aa462-1d86-5880-b6e2-2f6b1d79b41d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125813 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125814 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a multivariable function $f(x,y),$ the differential\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ntells us how infinitesimal changes in $x$ and $y,$ denoted $\\textrm dx$ and $\\textrm dy,$ result in an infinitesimal change in $f,$ denoted $\\textrm df.$\n\nConsequently, we can use the differential to *approximate* the change in $f$ as we move from one point to another. If we move from a point $(x,y)$ to a point $(x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y),$ then the approximate change in $f$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf(x,y) & ≈(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,Δx+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,Δy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above formula is very similar to the differential formula, except the $\\textrm d$'s are replaced with $\\Delta$'s. The $\\textrm d$'s refer to infinitesimal changes, while the $\\Delta$'s refer to changes that can actually be measured." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125814 #edb/ref 1065 4214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125814 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c2d5565a-cb57-537b-bd6e-64ebd0b91783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125814 #edb/ref 1067 "Approximating Change Using Differentials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282101 #edb/ref 1080 "-0.521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282101 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282101 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e9f4654-e89d-5d9e-a79c-0bb5f857defd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282298 #edb/ref 1080 "4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282298 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282298 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4828acff-c15d-5d8b-9aef-ef355a0e567c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282303 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282303 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282303 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "488bc018-0350-58c4-baa3-b73fb7849aec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282388 #edb/ref 1080 "1.18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282388 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282388 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5152f4ba-9807-5034-82d9-7afabc4856aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282464 #edb/ref 1080 "-2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282464 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282464 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b56108c-83c5-51b0-97bc-ef1c90c49d24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282755 #edb/ref 1080 "0.15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282755 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1759218604